; ============================================================
; TITLE:    INSTANT: One Key, One Command
; CATEGORY: Classic Logo
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     keyboard, READCHAR, young children, CASE
; SUMMARY:  Drive the turtle with single letters (F, B, R, L and friends), the way the youngest Logo users first met the turtle.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Each key you press is one turtle command, done at once. F walks
;   forward, R turns right, C changes the color, and so on. A help card
;   is written in the corner of the screen.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run, then CLICK THE TURTLE WORLD so it gets your key presses.
;     F  forward 30        B  back 30
;     R  right 30          L  left 30
;     U  pen up            D  pen down
;     C  next color        H  go home
;     S  stamp a turtle    E  erase everything
;     Q  quit
;   The arrow keys work too.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * READCHAR waits for one key and outputs it
;   * CASE picks what to do from a list of choices
;   * a loop that keeps going until you say stop
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Add a key T that turns the turtle around (right 180).
;   * Make F walk 10 steps instead of 30 for finer drawings.
;   * Add a key that draws a whole square in one go.
; ============================================================

; Very young children can drive the turtle before they can type whole
; words. Early Logo classrooms often gave them a little program like
; this one, usually called INSTANT: every key does one thing right away.
; Later the children learn that the same commands can be typed in full
; and saved as procedures of their own.

to help.card
  penup setxy -390 280 setheading 0
  setpencolor 15
  setlabelheight 14
  foreach [[F forward, B back] [R right, L left] [U pen up, D pen down] ~
           [C color, H home] [S stamp, E erase] [Q quit]] [
    label ?
    setxy -390 ycor - 18
  ]
  penup home pendown
end

to next.color
  make "colors lput first :colors butfirst :colors
  setpencolor first :colors
end

; DO.KEY carries out one key. CASE ignores upper and lower case, so f
; and F both work. Keys we do not know are ignored, so nothing bad can
; happen whatever a child presses.
to do.key :key
  case :key [
    [[f UP] forward 30]
    [[b DOWN] back 30]
    [[r RIGHT] right 30]
    [[l LEFT] left 30]
    [[u] penup]
    [[d] pendown]
    [[c] next.color]
    [[h] home]
    [[s] stamp]
    [[e] clean help.card]
    [else ignore :key]
  ]
end

to instant
  clearscreen
  setbackground [245 245 235]
  setpensize 3
  setshape "turtle
  make "colors [[30 90 220] [220 50 50] [40 160 70] [240 150 20] [150 60 200] [0 0 0]]
  help.card
  setpencolor first :colors
  showturtle
  print [Click the turtle world, then press F B R L U D C H S E. Q quits.]
  forever [
    localmake "key readchar
    ; READCHAR can give back an empty list if the keyboard is gone
    if emptyp :key [stop]
    if equalp :key "q [print [Bye!] stop]
    do.key :key
  ]
end

instant
